  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63166,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34984,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Shave biopsy showing a relatively symmetrical, exoendophytic lesion with central keratin-filled crater with overhanging lips of normal epidermis.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34984.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34984.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63167,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34985,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Predominant compact orthokeratin of trichilemmal type surrounded by cells with characteristic abundant glassy eosinophilic cytoplasm. Central well-formed neutrophil microabscess.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34985.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34985.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63168,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34986,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "The tumour is wrapped around a small nerve in the base of the biopsy. The peripheral (basal) cells retain a normal N:C&nbsp;ratio. Cellular atypia is mild.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34986.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34986.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63169,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34987,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Typical mosaic pattern of p16 expression by immunohistochemistry.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34987.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34987.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63170,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34988,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Peripheral graded pattern of p53 expression by immunohistochemistry.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34988.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34988.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63171,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34989,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Ki-67 showing a peripheral-only pattern, which is congruent with the pattern on p53 immunohistochemistry. This combination is typical for keratoacanthoma and uncommon in cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34989.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34989.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63172,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34991,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Clinical history: 79-year-old woman, nodule on shin, suspicious for keratoacanthoma or squamous cell carcinoma. Punch biopsy shows part of a well-formed crater with predominant laminated orthokeratosis forming rounded infundibular-type structures.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34991.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34991.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63173,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34992,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Deeper part shows nodules comprising the characteristic keratinocytes with abundant glassy cytoplasm and typical trichilemmal pattern of keratinization; parakeratosis is slight, without hyperchromatic crowded nuclei.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34992.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34992.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63174,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34993,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Cellular pleomorphism is mild to moderate, but cells retain a normal N:C&nbsp;ratio, and such large nuclei may be due to hyperdiploid cells (note multinucleated cell on the right). The lesion lacks the thick proliferative zone, high N:C&nbsp;ratio, and acantholysis that characterize squamous cell carcinoma.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34993.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34993.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63175,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34994,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Clinical image. After 4&nbsp;months of watchful waiting, the lesion was stable in size but had not resolved, and lesion was excised with clear margins.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34994.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34994.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63176,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34995,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Same case as in &lt;&lt;34994&gt;&gt; showing a fully regressed keratoacanthoma that retains the predominantly laminated and orthokeratinized infundibular-type rounded structures in the crater and some residua of neutrophilic debris. There are no longer any glassy keratinocytes, significant proliferation, or active inflammation, and the lesion resembles an infundibular epidermal cyst. The stromal fibrosis around the lesion is characteristic.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34995.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34995.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63177,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34997,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Clinical details: 83-year-old man, 30&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;25&nbsp;mm nodule on vertex of scalp, suspicious for squamous cell carcinoma. The tumour has a crater with infundibular connection superficially and a lack of actinic keratosis. Thick proliferative borders are clearly evident, and there is a relative lack of a marked inflammatory response, fairly prominent parakeratosis, and a relative lack of neutrophil microabscesses.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34997.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34997.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63178,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34998,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Thick, broad proliferative zones without any suggestion of regression are features supportive of a squamous cell carcinoma.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34998.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34998.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63179,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34999,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Elastic entrapment is present in the epithelium and in the keratin crater, but this is not specific for keratoacanthoma (EVG).",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/34999.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_34999.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63180,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35000,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Highly aberrant weak, mainly cytoplasmic, peripheral pattern (p16).",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35000.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35000.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63181,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35001,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Highly aberrant null pattern, which is specific for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (p53).",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35001.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35001.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63182,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35002,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Nuclear staining extends well beyond the peripheral one to three cell layers, and this feature has been found to be specific for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma and incompatible with keratoacanthoma (Ki-67).",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35002.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35002.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63183,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35004,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Clinical details: 77-year-old woman, lesion on chest, present 3&nbsp;months, suspicious for squamous cell carcinoma. Lesion metastasized to axillary lymph node at 18&nbsp;months following complete excision after a favoured diagnosis of keratoacanthoma. These architectural features are quite typical for keratoacanthoma, including an exo/endophytic nodule approaching the level of the sweat gland coils, well-formed crater, and organized multilocular infundibular-trichilemmal pattern with central maturation in all areas.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35004.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35004.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63184,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35005,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Slight hyperchromasia, increased N:C&nbsp;ratio, and thickening of the proliferative borders. Other features of concern were moderate parakeratosis, complete absence of neutrophil microabscesses, and a relative lack of regression at 3&nbsp;months.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35005.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35005.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63185,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35007,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "p16 shows an aberrant, largely null, focally weak and cytoplasmic pattern; note the typical reactive mosaic pattern in the adjacent epidermis and focally in the base of the tumour, which may be a connected reactive follicle (p16).",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35007.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35007.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63186,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35008,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "p53 shows upregulated, but peripheral graded, pattern that was congruent with a peripheral-only pattern on Ki-67. This is a feature fairly typical for keratoacanthoma, but occasionally seen in cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35008.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35008.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63187,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35009,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Lymph node deposit showing a moderately differentiated squamous cell carcinoma.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35009.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35009.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 63188,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 35010,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Squamoproliferative lesion of uncertain malignant potential, keratoacanthoma-like",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Highly aberrant null p16 in the lymph node metastasis. A few positive stromal cells and macrophages are noted.",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/35010.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/33/thumb_35010.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 64027,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34896,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Keratoacanthoma subtypes</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 100.0%; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"100%\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.04%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Subtype</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 74.96%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"74%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Clinical features</strong><u></u></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.04%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Giant keratoacanthoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 74.96%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"74%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&gt;&nbsp;50&nbsp;mm (may be as large as 200&nbsp;mm)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.04%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Keratoacanthoma centrifugum marginatum</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 74.96%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"74%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare sporadic subtype, with persistent radial growth and typically large (sometimes &gt;&nbsp;200&nbsp;mm), occasionally multiple, annular or serpiginous lesions, actively advancing borders, and central healing; may be associated with significant scarring</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.04%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Multiple self-healing epitheliomas of Ferguson Smith (MIM number: 132800)</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 74.96%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"74%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare; autosomal dominant inheritance; onset from first to seventh decade of life; variable penetrance; multiple follicular keratoacanthoma-like lesions with a predilection for the head and neck and the limbs <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{23358096; 33256177}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 25.04%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"25%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Generalized eruptive keratoacanthoma of Grzybowski</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 74.96%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"74%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Rare; usually non-familial; eruption typically in the fifth to seventh decade of life; hundreds to thousands of small (1&ndash;2&nbsp;mm), dome-shaped follicular papules with or without a keratotic centre; scarring not pronounced <span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{26853179}</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 64028,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 33,
    "fileId": 34901,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Histological features of keratoacanthoma and follicular (trichilemmal-infundibular) cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, keratoacanthoma-like (cSCC-KAL)</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Richard A. Carr",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 100.0%; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"100%\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Feature</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Keratoacanthoma</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">cSCC-KAL</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Symmetry</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Yes</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Exoendophytic</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Yes</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Even, bowl-shaped base</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Yes</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Keratotic crater</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Well formed in mature stage</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Overhanging lips of epidermis</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Well formed in mature stage</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 6; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Ratio of width to thickness</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Typically 2&ndash;3:1</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable, may be &lt;&nbsp;2:1 (verticality)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 7; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Borders of invasion in proliferative phase</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Usually infiltrative at the periphery, can be mainly circumscribed</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable, but often circumscribed and pushing</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 8; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Depth relative to sweat gland coils</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Usually at the same level; below uncommon</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable; may be high, at the same level, or below</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 9; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Predominant parakeratosis with hyperchromatic and crowded nuclei</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Minimal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Often present, but variable</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 10; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Infundibular keratinization: laminated with keratohyalin granules</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Predominates superficially and centrally</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Usually less orderly</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 11; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Trichilemmal keratinization: compact with deficient keratohyalin granules</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Predominates deeply and peripherally</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Usually less orderly</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 12; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">N:C&nbsp;ratio in peripheral cells</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Typically 1:1 and preserved in cells with large nuclei or multinucleation</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable, often reduced</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 13; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Mitotic activity beyond peripheral 1&ndash;3&nbsp;cells</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Minimal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Usually present</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 14; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Nuclear pleomorphism beyond peripheral 1&ndash;3&nbsp;cells</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Minimal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Usually present, may be focal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 15; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Spontaneous acantholysis (not associated with neutrophils)</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Absent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Frequent, may be focal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 16; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Intraepithelial &ldquo;follicular&rdquo; mucin</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Absent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Frequent, may be focal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 17; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Necrosis</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Absent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Uncommon</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 18; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Perineural invasion</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Uncommon (&lt;&nbsp;1%)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Uncommon</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 19; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Venous invasion</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Rare</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Rare</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 20; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Elastic and collagen entrapment (H&amp;E or elastic stains)</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Common, often widespread, occasionally absent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable, often absent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 21; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Band-like, lichenoid tissue reaction, lymphocytes predominant, variable eosinophils and neutrophils</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Often prominent in mature stage but lacking in early proliferative lesions</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable, can be prominent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 22; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Plasma cells</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Minimal</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable, may be prominent</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 23; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Regression: loss of proliferative zone (squamatization), numerous Civatte and colloid bodies, peripheral fibrosis, keratin granulomas</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Established regression supports diagnosis</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Variable but usually lacking</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 24; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Desmoplastic stroma</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Peripheral to lesion in late regressing stage</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Variable but often lacking</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 25; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Severe solar elastosis</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Uncommon</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">Common</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 26; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Directly associated actinic keratosis</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.34%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Rare</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 33.32%; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"33%\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Uncommon</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
